on but also  encouraging um to see that sign of life  you said you've had no communication  with others in mariopol are you hearing  anything about the situation there from  others  yeah i didn't manage to reach any of my  close people however my friend's parents  called here yesterday they managed to  find some spot in the center of the city  where there was some weak mobile network  and they shared that the  city is being shelled  every hour every minute  and i heard from telegram channel you  know there are some groups so when  people  communicate and exchange some  information between them so i heard that  my neighborhood where my parents live  it was really damaged yesterday and  today and uh the bomb hit my house  where my parents have an apartment and i  have no idea whether my apartment is  still there or it's completely destroyed  so  this is terrifying  and maria you're in levive now  what is your plan from there  well i'm still here i still have no  plans to leave the country within the  nearest future and i hope that the  situation at least here will remain a  bit stable as it is now and we continue  helping our people for instance today we  were again on the railway station trying  to get some humanitarian stuff to to  kiev  and  there are a lot of people here and  everyone  wants to  take part in some volunteering  initiatives and help each other  maria we're going to leave it there  thank you again for joining us on dw  news and please take care  thank you  and the un says more than 2.5 million  refugees have fled to other countries  since the invasion of ukraine began the  majority of those people 1.5 million  according to polish border authorities  have headed to poland others have made  their way to hungary slovakia moldova  and romania more than a hundred thousand  have also entered russia  and i'm now joined by dw correspondent  brigitte shulka she joins me from poland  near the border with ukraine hi welcome  to the show thanks for joining us um